# `for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; --i) { const x = a[i]; if (x > 1) s += x; }`: the list under the counter is asked in front of the loop, and where that fails the turns that are left run in a copy of the loop that asks the counter alone.
# Only the ways out of the typed copy lead to that copy, and they were rare. What only rare edges lead to is a rare region to the back end: "hardly ever runs, nothing is paid for it":
# a loop whose head lies in one is not turned, and a slow call inside one is no rare region of its own, so the counter, which lives across it, got a slot: stored at the head and read back at the step, in every turn: 38 instructions a turn where the same turn takes 33.
# The rest of a loop's turns run there once a guess has failed: that is unlikely, not rare. The entry of such a copy is unlikely now, and so is each block that enters it.
